{
  "problem_statement": "*** Problem Statement\n\n# Nilpotent Orbit Induction for Classical Groups\n\n## Setup\n\nLet $F$ be a $p$-adic field (a finite extension of $\\mathbb{Q}_p$).\n\nLet $(V, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ be a non-degenerate formed space over $F$: that is, $V$ is a finite-dimensional $F$-vector space and $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is a non-degenerate bilinear form on $V$ that is either **symmetric** ($\\epsilon = +1$) or **alternating** ($\\epsilon = -1$). Let $G = \\mathrm{Isom}(V, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ be the isometry group:\n- If $\\epsilon = +1$: $G = \\mathrm{O}(V)$ (orthogonal group), with $\\dim V = N$.\n- If $\\epsilon = -1$: $G = \\mathrm{Sp}(V)$ (symplectic group), with $\\dim V = 2n$.\n\nLet $\\mathfrak{g} = \\mathrm{Lie}(G)$ and let $\\mathcal{N}(\\mathfrak{g})$ denote the nilpotent cone of $\\mathfrak{g}$.\n\n### Parameterization of rational nilpotent orbits via $\\mathfrak{sl}_2$-triples\n\nLet $e \\in \\mathcal{N}(\\mathfrak{g})(F)$ be a rational nilpotent element. By the Jacobson–Morozov theorem, $e$ extends to an $\\mathfrak{sl}_2$-triple $\\{e, h, f\\} \\subset \\mathfrak{g}(F)$. The triple defines an $\\mathfrak{sl}_2(F)$-module structure on $V$. Decompose $V$ into isotypic components:\n\\[\nV = \\bigoplus_{i \\geq 1} V_i \\otimes W_i,\n\\]\nwhere $W_i$ is the $i$-dimensional irreducible representation of $\\mathfrak{sl}_2(F)$, and $V_i$ is the **multiplicity space** (a finite-dimensional $F$-vector space, with $V_i = 0$ for all but finitely many $i$). The constraint is\n\\[\n\\sum_{i \\geq 1} i \\cdot \\dim_F V_i = \\dim_F V = N.\n\\]\n\nThe form $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ on $V$ induces a non-degenerate form on each multiplicity space $V_i$: using the unique (up to scalar) $\\mathfrak{sl}_2$-invariant bilinear form on $W_i$, one obtains a non-degenerate bilinear form $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i$ on $V_i$ of parity\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i = \\epsilon \\cdot (-1)^{i-1}.\n\\]\nConcretely:\n- If $V$ is **symplectic** ($\\epsilon = -1$): $V_i$ is symplectic when $i$ is odd, and symmetric (i.e., orthogonal) when $i$ is even.\n- If $V$ is **orthogonal** ($\\epsilon = +1$): $V_i$ is symmetric when $i$ is odd, and alternating (i.e., symplectic) when $i$ is even. In particular, $\\dim V_i$ is even when $i$ is even.\n\nA **rational nilpotent orbit** of $G(F)$ on $\\mathcal{N}(\\mathfrak{g})(F)$ is uniquely determined by the collection of formed spaces\n\\[\n\\{(V_i, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i)\\}_{i \\geq 1},\n\\]\nwhere each $(V_i, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i)$ is a non-degenerate formed space over $F$ of the parity $\\epsilon_i$ prescribed above, subject to the compatibility condition that $\\bigoplus_i V_i \\otimes W_i$ reconstructs $(V, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ as formed space.\n\nTwo such collections give the same rational orbit if and only if the formed spaces $(V_i, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i)$ are isometric for every $i$.\n\n\nOver $F$, the rational refinement is encoded by the isometry classes of the formed spaces $\\{(V_i, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i)\\}$. We write the rational orbit as $\\mathcal{O}_{\\{(V_i, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i)\\}}$, or $\\mathcal{O}_{\\lambda, \\epsilon}$ for short, where $\\lambda$ is the formed partition and $\\epsilon$ collectively denotes the rational invariants (isometry classes of the multiplicity spaces).\n\n\n### Maximal parabolic subgroups\n\nA **maximal rational parabolic subgroup** $P$ of $G = \\mathrm{Isom}(V)$ arises from a totally isotropic subspace $X \\subset V$ defined over $F$. Let $r = \\dim_F X$ and $X^\\perp$ be its orthogonal complement. Then $V_0 = X^\\perp / X$ inherits a non-degenerate form of the same type as $V$. The parabolic $P = \\mathrm{Stab}_G(X)$ has a Levi decomposition $P = LU$, where $U$ is the unipotent radical and the Levi factor is\n\\[\nL \\cong \\mathrm{GL}(X) \\times G_0, \\qquad G_0 = \\mathrm{Isom}(V_0, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_{V_0}).\n\\]\nHere $\\mathrm{GL}(X) \\cong \\mathrm{GL}_r(F)$ and $G_0$ is a group of the same type as $G$ but of smaller rank.\n\n### Nilpotent orbit induction from a maximal parabolic\n\nLet $P = LU$ be a maximal rational parabolic of $G$ as above, with $L \\cong \\mathrm{GL}_r(F) \\times G_0$. Given a rational nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_L$ in $\\mathfrak{l}(F) = \\mathrm{Lie}(L)(F)$, the **induced rational nilpotent orbit** $\\mathrm{Ind}_L^G(\\mathcal{O}_L)$ is defined as follows. Consider the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal{O}_L + \\mathfrak{u}(F) = \\{e + x : e \\in \\mathcal{O}_L,\\, x \\in \\mathfrak{u}(F)\\} \\subset \\mathfrak{g}(F),\n\\]\nwhere $\\mathfrak{u} = \\mathrm{Lie}(U)$. Then there exists a unique rational nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}$ in $\\mathfrak{g}(F)$ that is open dense (in the $p$-adic topology) in the closure of $G(F) \\cdot (\\mathcal{O}_L + \\mathfrak{u}(F))$. We define $\\mathrm{Ind}_L^G(\\mathcal{O}_L) := \\mathcal{O}$.\n\nThe definition depends only on $L$ (and the orbit $\\mathcal{O}_L$), not on the choice of parabolic $P$ with Levi factor $L$.\n\n---\n\n## Problem\n\nGiven a rational nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_{G_0}$ of $G_0 = \\mathrm{Isom}(V_0)$, we regard it as the nilpotent orbit $\\{0\\} \\times \\mathcal{O}_{G_0}$ in the Levi $L \\cong \\mathrm{GL}_r(F) \\times G_0$ (i.e., the $\\mathrm{GL}(X)$-component is the zero orbit).\n\nWe write this induction as\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Ind}_{G_0}^G(\\mathcal{O}_{G_0}) := \\mathrm{Ind}_L^G(\\{0\\} \\times \\mathcal{O}_{G_0}).\n\\]\n\nUsing the parameterization above, the rational nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_{G_0}$ on $G_0 = \\mathrm{Isom}(V_0)$ is given by the collection of formed multiplicity spaces $\\{(V_i^0, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i^0)\\}_{i \\geq 1}$ with $\\sum_i i \\cdot \\dim V_i^0 = \\dim V_0$.\n\n### Algorithm for induction $\\mathrm{Ind}_{G_0}^G$\n\nLet $P = LU$ be a maximal parabolic with $L \\cong \\mathrm{GL}_r(F) \\times G_0$ (arising from a totally isotropic subspace $X$ of dimension $r$). Let $\\mathcal{O}_{G_0}$ be a rational nilpotent orbit of $G_0 = \\mathrm{Isom}(V_0)$, given by the collection of formed multiplicity spaces $\\{(V_i^0, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i^0)\\}_{i \\geq 1}$.\n\n**Give an explicit combinatorial algorithm** to compute the rational nilpotent orbit $\\mathrm{Ind}_{G_0}^G(\\mathcal{O}_{G_0})$ in terms of the formed multiplicity spaces. That is, determine the collection $\\{(V'_i, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i)\\}_{i \\geq 1}$ parameterizing the induced orbit from $\\{(V_i^0, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_i^0)\\}_{i \\geq 1}$ and the integer $r$.\n\n**Prove** that your algorithm correctly computes the induced orbit.\n",
